\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Dihedral symmetry refines the four-cycle sectors}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{Take the [cycle](fdip-004W) with #{n=4}, #{w=m=1}.
Let #{D_4=\langle r,s:r^4=s^2=1,\ srs=r^{-1}\rangle}, of
order 8, act on vertices by #{r(v)=v+1}, #{s(v)=-v}.
On the oriented edges #{e_v:v\to v+1}, rotation sends
#{e_v} to #{e_{v+1}} with positive sign, while reflection sends
#{e_v} to #{e_{-v-1}} with negative sign. The latter edge runs
from #{-v-1} to #{-v}, the reverse of the transported endpoints.
The 4 rotations restore each edge; two reflections restore its label
and give positive sign. Reflecting, rotating, then reflecting sends
#{e_v} to #{e_{v-1}} with two negative signs, hence agrees with the
inverse rotation. The vertex maps obey the same relations, so these
maps define a signed endpoint action. This uses the dihedral
presentation in \citet{Section 5.3, p. 36}{serre1977linear};
the edge signs and precision calculation are explicit here.}

\texfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.25,
  edge/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},semithick}]
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=2pt] (a) at (0,0) {0};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=2pt] (b) at (2,0) {1};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=2pt] (c) at (2,2) {2};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=2pt] (d) at (0,2) {3};
\draw[edge] (a) -- node[below=3pt] {$e_0$} (b);
\draw[edge] (b) -- node[right=3pt] {$e_1$} (c);
\draw[edge] (c) -- node[above=3pt] {$e_2$} (d);
\draw[edge] (d) -- node[left=3pt] {$e_3$} (a);
\draw[dashed] (-0.3,-0.3) -- (a.south west);
\draw[dashed] (a.north east) -- (c.south west);
\draw[dashed] (c.north east) -- (2.3,2.3);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The dashed diagonal is the reflection axis: #{s} fixes vertices
#{0,2}, exchanges #{1,3}, and sends #{e_0} to #{e_3} with reversed
orientation. All 4 weights and the mass are one.}

\p{An orthonormal basis, grouped by real irreducible type, is
##{\begin{aligned}
u&=(1,1,1,1)/2,&a&=(1,-1,1,-1)/2,\\
c&=(1,0,-1,0)/\sqrt2,&b&=(0,1,0,-1)/\sqrt2.
\end{aligned}}
The line #{\mathbb Ru} is trivial. On #{\mathbb Ra}, rotation
acts by #{-1} and reflection by #{+1}; call this the alternating
line. On #{K=\operatorname{span}(c,b)},
##{\rho(r)|_K=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\rho(s)|_K=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
There is no real rotation-invariant line, so #{K} is irreducible.
A real matrix commuting with the rotation is #{x\bbI+y\rho(r)};
commuting also with the reflection forces #{y=0}. Thus its
commuting algebra is #{\mathbb R \bbI}, the real-type case.
The two distinct lines and #{K} are pairwise nonisomorphic, each
occurring once.}

\p{Direct substitution in the cycle precision gives
##{Qu=u,\qquad Qa=5a,\qquad Qc=3c,\qquad Qb=3b.}
The three orthogonal projectors are #{uu^T}, #{aa^T}, and
#{cc^T+bb^T=\bbI-uu^T-aa^T}; their ranges have dimensions #{1,1,2}
and precision eigenvalues #{1,5,3}, respectively.
For the subgroup generated by #{r^2}, the positive sector is
#{\operatorname{span}(u,a)} and the negative sector is #{K}.
The [half-turn calculation](fdip-003S) therefore has two
#{2\times2} blocks, whereas the full dihedral action separates
its positive block into two lines. Changing the acting group changes
the isotypic decomposition without changing this precision.}
